title: "Journal, Ledger & Trial Balance — the Accounting Cycle",
subject: "Accounting",
tag: "Paper 1 · Category A · Very high weightage",
blurb: "Source document → journal → ledger → trial balance: the full cycle, debit–credit rules, and the errors a trial balance cannot catch.",
intro: "Every rupee a business records travels the same road: a source document (cash memo, invoice, receipt) proves the transaction, the <strong>journal</strong> records it first in date order, the <strong>ledger</strong> re-files it account by account, and the <strong>trial balance</strong> checks the arithmetic before final accounts are drawn. The journal is the book of <em>original</em> entry, the ledger the book of <em>secondary</em> entry, and the trial balance is a list — not an account — that only proves debits equal credits. Mastering this cycle is Category A material: it underpins everything from final accounts to depreciation adjustments.",
